O|
T,M|               FADE IN (BEFORE TITLES)
O|
S|               EXT. FRENCH COUNTRYSIDE -- DUSK
O|
N|               Silence -- complete silence for the urbanite, though the 
N|               oncoming darkness is punctuated by the sounds of farm country -- 
N|               a few birds, a distant rumble of thunder from some heavy 
N|               clouds on the horizon, a dog's barking.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA PANS the green, squared-off flatland, lit only by a 
N|               fine sunset in its final throes. Then, gradually, starting 
N|               from nothing, a rumble is heard, quickly growing louder and 
N|               louder until the sound of a train can be recognized.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA PANS quickly, discovering the railroad line atop a 
N|               man-made rise of land, and the speeding passenger train is 
N|               upon us, flashing by with a roar.
O|
N|               Then, as if from nowhere, the figure of a man hits the 
N|               embankment and rolls crazily down to the bottom into the 
N|               thick underbrush alongside the tracks.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- BODY
O|
N|               It lies in the bushes, still, unmoving -- dead. CAMERA PANS 
N|               AWAY to the quiet peaceful countryside as the sound of the 
N|               train fades off until there is silence once more.
O|
N|               TITLE MUSIC begins with a crash.
O|
M|                                      (MAIN TITLES)
O|
D|               DELETED
O|
T|               FADE IN:
O|
S|               EXT. MEGEVE -- DAY
O|
N|               A handsome and elegant hotel perched on the mountain-side 
N|               overlooking the French resort town. A large, open sun deck -- 
N|               tables, gaily colored parasols, sun bathers.
O|
N|               One of the latter is REGINA LAMPERT, a lovely young girl.
O|
N|               She is, besides taking in the sun, involved in her favorite 
N|               activity -- eating.
O|
N,G|               Then -- a dark, ominous shape intrudes in the f.g. FOCUS 
N,G|               CHANGES to bring into sharp relief a revolver -- shining, 
N|               black and ugly in the sunlight.
O|
N|               REGGIE, unaware of her danger, continues to eat.
O|
N|               The finger tightens around the trigger and finally the gun 
N|               shoots -- a stream of water arcs, with unerring aim, straight 
N|               into REGGIE's face.
O|
N,G|               ANOTHER ANGLE
O|
N,G|               Including JEAN-LOUIS, a French boy of six or so. REGGIE looks 
N|               at him sternly.
O|
C|                                     JEAN-LOUIS
P|                              (in for trouble)
U|                         Oh, la.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Don't tell me you didn't know it was 
U|                         loaded.
P|                              (calling)
U|                         Sylvie!
O|
N,G|               WIDER ANGLE
O|
N|               SYLVIE GAUDET, French, attractive, blonde, in her early 
N|               thirties, comes from the railing of the sun deck to join 
N|               REGGIE and JEAN-LOUIS.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Isn't there something constructive 
U|                         he can do -- like start an avalanche?
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
P|                              (to JEAN-LOUIS)
U|                         Va jouer, mon ange.
O|
N|               JEAN-LOUIS scampers off, content to have gotten off so 
N|               lightly. SYLVIE notices REGGIE's lunch which consists of 
N|               cold chicken, potato salad, rolls and butter, wine and coffee.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         When you start to eat like this 
U|                         something is the matter.
O|
N|               No answer from REGGIE. SYLVIE begins reading a magazine as 
N|               REGGIE continues eating.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Sylvie -- I'm getting a divorce.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         Ça alors! From Charles?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         He's the only husband I've got. I 
U|                         tried to make it work, I really have -- 
U|                         but --
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         But what?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I don't know how to explain it. I'm 
U|                         just too miserable.
O|
N|               REGGIE picks up a chicken leg and starts off. SYLVIE regards 
N|               the devastated table before following.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         It is infuriating that your 
U|                         unhappiness does not turn to fat!
O|
S|               INT. SWIMMING POOL -- DAY
O|
N|               A magnificent indoor, glass-enclosed pool, the vista of snow-
N|               covered mountains seen through the ceiling-high windows 
N|               beyond. REGGIE and SYLVIE are passing through, their 
N|               conversation continuing.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         But why do you want a divorce?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Because I don't love him.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         But that is no reason to get a 
U|                         divorce!
O|
S|               EXT. HOTEL TERRACE -- DAY
O|
N|               An open balcony running around two sides of the pool, sun-
N|               worshippers lying in deck-chairs. REGGIE and SYLVIE appear, 
N|               their conversation continuing.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         With a rich husband and this year's 
U|                         clothes you will not find it difficult 
U|                         to make some new friends.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (sitting)
U|                         I admit I moved to Paris because I 
U|                         was tired of American Provincial,
O|
O|                                      
U|                         but that doesn't mean I'm ready for 
U|                         French Traditional. I loathe the 
U|                         idea of divorce, Sylvie, but -- if 
U|                         only Charles had been honest with me -- 
U|                         that's all I ask of anybody -- the 
U|                         simple truth. But with him, everything 
U|                         is secrecy and lies. He's hiding 
U|                         something -- something frightening -- 
U|                         something terrible -- and evil.
O|
N|               She stops as she is aware of a weird figure hovering over 
N|               her. She wheels, terrified.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- PERUVIAN SNOW-MASK
O|
N|               A strange, grotesque knitted mask that completely covers the 
N|               face except for eyes, nose and mouth. The eyes inside this 
N|               particular mask stare down at REGGIE.
O|
C|                                     MAN
U|                         Does this belong to you?
O|
N,G|               CAMERA PANS down to include JEAN-LOUIS, his hand held firmly 
N|               by the man in the mask.
O|
N,G|               WIDER ANGLE
O|
N,G|               Including REGGIE, MAN, SYLVIE and JEAN-LOUIS. REGGIE is too 
N|               terrified to answer. Realizing this, the man, PETER JOSHUA, 
N|               takes off the snow-mask to reveal a handsome, tanned face.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Oh, forgive me.
P|                              (indicating JEAN-LOUIS)
U|                         Is this yours?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (indicating SYLVIE)
U|                         It's hers. Where'd you find him, 
U|                         robbing a bank?
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         He was throwing snowballs at Baron 
U|                         Rothschild.
P|                              (a pause)
U|                         We don't know each other, do we?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Why, do you think we're going to?
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         I don't know -- how would I know?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I'm afraid I already know a great 
U|                         many people. Until one of them dies 
U|                         I couldn't possibly meet anyone else.
O|
C|                                     PETER
P|                              (smiling)
U|                         Yes, of course. But you will let me 
U|                         know if anyone goes on the critical 
U|                         list
P|                              (he starts off)
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Quitter.
O|
C|                                     PETER
P|                              (turning)
U|                         How's that?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         You give up awfully easy, don't you?
O|
N|               Eyeing one, then the other, SYLVIE sizes up the situation 
N|               and rises.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         Viens, Jean-Louis, let us take a 
U|                         walk. I have never seen a Rothschild 
U|                         before.
O|
N|               SYLVIE and JEAN-LOUIS start off, but not before the boy 
N|               squirts PETER with his pistol.
O|
C|                                     PETER
P|                              (drying)
U|                         Clever fellow -- almost missed me.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I'm afraid you're blocking my view.
O|
C|                                     PETER
P|                              (moving)
U|                         Sorry. Which view would you like?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         The one you're blocking. This is the 
U|                         last chance I have -- I'm flying 
U|                         back to Paris this afternoon. What's 
U|                         your name?
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Peter Joshua.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I'm Regina Lampert.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Is there a Mr. Lampert?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Yes.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Good for you.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         No, it isn't. I'm getting a divorce.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Please, not on my account.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         No, you see, I don't really love 
U|                         him.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Well, you're honest, anyway.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Yes, I am -- I'm compulsive about it -- 
U|                         dishonesty infuriates me. Like when 
U|                         you go into a drugstore.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         I'm not sure I --
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Well, you go in and you ask for some 
U|                         toothpaste -- the small size -- and 
U|                         the man brings you the large size. 
U|                         You tell him you wanted the small 
U|                         size but he says the large size is 
U|                         the small size. I always thought the 
U|                         large size was the largest size, but 
U|                         he says that the family size, the 
U|                         economy size and the giant size are 
U|                         all larger than the large size -- 
U|                         that the large size is the smallest 
U|                         size there is.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Oh. I guess.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Is there a Mrs. Joshua?
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Yes, but we're divorced.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         That wasn't a proposal -- I was just 
U|                         curious.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Is your husband with you?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Oh, Charles is hardly ever with me. 
U|                         First it was separate rooms -- now 
U|                         we're trying it with cities. What do 
U|                         people call you -- Pete?
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Mr. Joshua.
P|                              (turning to go)
U|                         Well, I've enjoyed talking with you.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Now you're angry.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         No, I'm not -- I've got some packing 
U|                         to do. I'm also going back to Paris 
U|                         today.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Oh. Well, wasn't it Shakespeare who 
U|                         said: "When strangers do meet they 
U|                         should erelong see one another again"?
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Shakespeare never said that.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         How do you know?
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         It's terrible -- you just made it 
U|                         up.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Well, the idea's right, anyway. Are 
U|                         you going to call me?
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Are you in the book?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Charles is.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         Is there only one Charles Lampert?
O|
D|               DELETED
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- REGGIE
O|
N|               Her face clouding.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Lord, I hope so.
O|
S|               EXT. AVENUE FOCH -- LAMPERT APARTMENT HOUSE -- DAY
O|
S|               The Arc de Triomphe at the far end of the Avenue. CAMERA 
N|               PANS to pick up a TAXI as it pulls up before the handsome 
N|               building. Inside are REGGIE, SYLVIE and JEAN-LOUIS.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- TAXI -- LAMPERT APARTMENT HOUSE
O|
N|               As REGGIE climbs out and the DRIVER begins unloading her 
N|               suitcases.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Goodbye, Sylvie, and thanks.
P|                              (She turns toward the 
P|                              house)
O|
N|               JEAN-LOUIS sticks his head out of the taxi window.
O|
C|                                     JEAN-LOUIS
U|                         When you get your divorce will you 
U|                         be going back to America?
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- THE TAXI
O|
N|               REGGIE looks at SYLVIE, surprised.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         He knows everything.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (to JEAN-LOUIS)
U|                         Don't you want me to stay?
O|
C|                                     JEAN-LOUIS
U|                         Yes, of course -- but if you went 
U|                         back and wrote me a letter --
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         -- you could have the stamps. I'll 
U|                         get you some here, okay?
O|
C|                                     JEAN-LOUIS
U|                         Okay.
O|
N|               REGGIE walks toward the house with the driver, who carries 
N|               her cases.  She presses the button that electrically opens 
N|               the front door.
O|
D|               DELETED
O|
S|               INT. APARTMENT LANDING -- DAY
O|
N|               As the elevator rises REGGIE gets out, followed by the driver.  
N|               He puts down the bags in front of the apartment door.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (handing him a tip)
U|                         Merci.
O|
N|               The driver leaves. She goes to the door and presses the 
N|               minuterie, the button that turns on the time-light, and the 
N|               lights come on. Then she rings the doorbell. There is no 
N|               answer. She rings again. Still nothing. Sighing, she digs 
N|               out her keys and starts to fit it into the lock. At this 
N|               moment the minuterie expires, plunging the scene into 
N|               darkness.
O|
C,E|                                     REGGIE'S VOICE
U|                         Wonderful.
O|
N|               She finds the button and the light goes on again. She inserts 
N|               the key and turns it.
O|
S|               INT. LAMPERT APARTMENT -- ENTRANCE HALL -- DAY
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- DOOR as it opens and REGGIE steps into the 
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT.
O|
N|               She stops, her expression changing.
O|
N,G|               REVERSE SHOT
O|
N,G|               From REGGIE's p.o.v. as CAMERA PANS the entrance hall. It is 
N|               bare -- no furniture, no rug, no pictures, no nothing.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- REGGIE
O|
N|               She stares for a moment, then goes back out into the landing.
O|
S|               INT. APARTMENT LANDING -- DAY
O|
N|               As REGGIE steps back outside. She looks at the nameplate 
N|               beside the door.
O|
T|               INSERT NAMEPLATE
O|
N|               It reads "MR. AND MRS. CHARLES LAMPERT."
O|
S|               INT. APARTMENT LANDING -- DAY
O|
N|               REGGIE looks at the plate in disbelief, then turns and hurries 
N|               back into the apartment.
O|
S|               INT. LAMPERT APARTMENT -- DAY
O|
N|               As REGGIE hurries into the entrance hall.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Honorine -- !
O|
N|               No answer.
O|
N|               Now, CAMERA FOLLOWING, she goes into the Salon. It is also 
N|               empty -- stripped bare. There are squares of the wall's 
N|               original color where paintings used to hang, the hooks still 
N|               in the wall.
O|
N|               She rushes now, going into the bedroom, CAMERA FOLLOWING 
N|               crazily, lurching and careening behind her. The bedroom, 
N|               too, is empty. She goes to the built-in wardrobe closets and 
N|               throws open all the doors. Only some hangers remain.
O|
N|               She pulls open the drawers -- nothing!
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Charles -- !
O|
N|               She turns, and running now, goes through another door to the 
N|               library, CAMERA FOLLOWING. The rows of shelves are as empty 
N|               as the rest of the apartment. She begins to turn in a circle, 
N|               looking for something, anything. In a panic she turns and 
N|               runs out, colliding suddenly with a MAN whom she (and we) 
N|               have not noticed until the moment of impact.
O|
N|               REGGIE screams.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- INSPECTOR GRANDPIERRE
O|
N|               A heavy-set man of no particular age with tobacco-colored 
N|               hair, and thick glasses.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         Madame Charles Lampert?
O|
N,G|               WIDER ANGLE
O|
N,G|               Including REGGIE, in a state of near-shock.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Yes.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         I am Inspector Edouard Grandpierre 
U|                         of the Police Judiciaire. Would you 
U|                         be so kind as to come with me, please?
O|
S|               INT. MORGUE -- DAY
O|
N,G|               We see a large metal drawer being opened and an all-too- 
N|               familiar shape outlined under a damp sheet of muslin.
O|
N,G|               ANOTHER ANGLE -- OVERHEAD
O|
N|               Looking straight down at the tops of REGGIE's, GRANDPIERRE's 
N|               and an ATTENDANT's head and smack into the open drawer.
O|
N|               GRANDPIERRE lifts a corner of the sheet at the bottom and 
N|               reveals a bare foot with a ticket tied to its big toe.
O|
N|               He stoops to read it. Satisfied, he recovers the foot, then 
N|               moves to the other end to uncover the head.  As the sheet 
N|               starts to lift:
O|
G|               REVERSE SHOT
O|
N,G|               REGGIE as she looks down into the CAMERA. She closes her 
N|               eyes for a moment, then looks again.
O|
C,E|                                     GRANDPIERRE'S VOICE (O.S.)
U|                         Well, Madame -- ?
O|
N|               She nods.
O|
C,E|                                     GRANDPIERRE'S VOICE (O.S.)
U|                         You are positive?
O|
N,G|               She nods again. GRANDPIERRE moves into the SHOT.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         You loved him?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I'm very cold.
O|
N|               GRANDPIERRE nods as he turns to the unseen ATTENDANT.
O|
N,G|               CAMERA suddenly moves as the 'drawer' is slid back into the 
N|               wall. BLACKNESS comes with a loud clang and continues while 
N|               the echo dies.
O|
S|               INT. GRANDPIERRE'S OFFICE -- DAY
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- DESK DRAWER (FROM ABOVE) as it is pulled open.
O|
N|               A photograph of Charles Lampert lies face up in the drawer.
O|
N|               A hand reaches in and pulls it out.
O|
N,G|               WIDER ANGLE
O|
N,G|               Including GRANDPIERRE sitting behind his desk, and REGGIE, 
N|               sitting across from him. The office is as bare as most 
N|               policemen's offices. GRANDPIERRE studies the photo.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         We discovered your husband's body 
U|                         lying next to the tracks of the Paris-
U|                         Bourdeaux railroad line. He was 
U|                         dressed only in his pajamas. Do you 
U|                         know of any reason why he might have 
U|                         wished to leave France?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Leave?
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         Your husband possessed a ticket of 
U|                         passage on the 'Maranguape.' It sailed 
U|                         from Bordeaux for Maracaibo this 
U|                         morning at seven.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (a pause)
U|                         I'm very confused.
O|
N|               She starts to rummage through her bag. GRANDPIERRE shoves a 
N|               package of French cigarettes across the desk to her. But she 
N|               pulls a package of nuts out of her bag. She begins separating 
N|               the shells with her thumb nail and eating the nuts, depositing 
N|               the shells in the ashtray. GRANDPIERRE watches this for an 
N|               instant.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         He was American?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Swiss.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         Oh. Swiss. His profession?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         He didn't have one.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         He was a wealthy man?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I don't know. I suppose so.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         About how wealthy would you say?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I don't know.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         Where did he keep his money?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I don't know.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         Besides yourself, who is his nearest 
U|                         relation?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I don't know.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
P|                              (exploding)
U|                         C'est absurde, Madame. To-tale-ment 
U|                         absurde!
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I know.
P|                              (pause)
U|                         I'm sorry.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         It is all right.
O|
N|               GRANDPIERRE sighs, puts down his pencil and pushes a button 
N|               on the desk. He removes a cigar from his desk and inserts it 
N|               into his mouth.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         Is it all right?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I wish you wouldn't.
O|
N|               He rips the cigar out of his mouth and slams it back into 
N|               the drawer, closing it fiercely. A UNIFORMED POLICEMAN sticks 
N|               his head in the door.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         Les effets de Lampert.
O|
N|               The POLICEMAN leaves and closes the door.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         On Wednesday last your husband sold 
U|                         the entire contents of the apartment 
U|                         at public auction. Furniture, 
U|                         clothing, kitchenware -- everything. 
U|                         The gallery, in complying with his 
U|                         wishes, paid him in cash. One million 
U|                         two hundred and fifty thousand New 
U|                         Francs. In dollars, a quarter of a 
U|                         million. The authorities in Bordeaux 
U|                         have searched his compartment on the 
U|                         train. They have searched it 
U|                         thoroughly. They did not find 
U|                         $250,000, Madame.
O|
N|               He opens the desk drawer, puts the cigar back in his mouth 
N|               and lights a match by scratching it against the glass desk-
N|               top before he remembers REGGIE's request. He puts it back in 
N|               the drawer again. The door opens and the POLICEMAN enters 
N|               again, this time carrying a wicker basket which he deposits 
N|               on GRANDPIERRE's desk, and leaves. GRANDPIERRE peers into 
N|               the basket.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         These few things are all that was 
U|                         found in the train compartment. There 
U|                         was no other baggage. Your husband 
U|                         must have been in a great hurry.
O|
N|               He begins to take them out, placing them on the desk, 
N|               identifying each item as he does.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         One wallet containing four thousand 
U|                         francs -- one agenda --
P|                              (pausing, he opens 
P|                              the notebook)
U|                         -- his last notation was made 
U|                         yesterday -- Thursday --
P|                              (reading)
U|                         "Five p.m. -- Jardin des Champs-
U|                         Elysées"
P|                              (looking up)
U|                         Why there?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I don't know. Perhaps he met somebody.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
P|                              (dryly)
U|                         Obviously.
P|                              (returning to the 
P|                              items in the basket)
U|                         One ticket of passage to South America -- 
U|                         one letter, stamped but unsealed, 
U|                         addressed to you --
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (lighting up)
U|                         A letter? May I see it?
O|
N|               GRANDPIERRE hands her the letter and watches her closely as 
N|               she reads it.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (reading)
U|                         "My dear Regina: I hope you are 
U|                         enjoying your holiday. Megeve can be 
U|                         so lovely this time of year. The 
U|                         days pass very slowly and I hope to 
U|                         see you soon. As always, Charles. 
U|                         P.S. Your dentist called yesterday. 
U|                         Your appointment has been changed."
P|                              (she looks up, puzzled)
U|                         Not very much, is it?
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         We took the liberty of calling your 
U|                         dentist -- we thought, perhaps, we 
U|                         would learn something.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Did you?
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         Yes. Your appointment has been 
U|                         changed.
P|                              (he smiles at his 
P|                              little joke, then 
P|                              returns to the basket)
U|                         One key to your apartment -- one 
U|                         comb -- one fountain pen --  one 
U|                         toothbrush -- one tin of tooth powder
P|                              (he looks up)
U|                         -- that is all.
O|
N|               He slides a sheet of paper and pen across to her, then starts 
N|               to put the things back into the basket while he speaks:
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         If you will sign this list you may 
U|                         take the things with you.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (sighing)
U|                         Is that all? Can I go now?
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
