S|               INT. FUNERAL CHAPEL -- DAY
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT of a phonograph. A hand appears, starts the record 
N|               on it spinning, then places the arm at the beginning.
O|
N|               An instant later ORGAN MUSIC starts with a roar.
O|
S|               INT. FUNERAL CHAPEL -- DAY
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT of the coffin. It rests on a low platform, with a 
N|               bouquet or two of flowers near the head, the lid open.
O|
N|               Inside, the face made up to look lifelike (but failing), lie 
N|               the remnants of Charles Lampert.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- GRANDPIERRE
O|
N|               The INSPECTOR sits quietly, eyes downcast, staring at his 
N,G|               hands in a prayer-like attitude. CAMERA PULLS BACK, revealing 
N|               row after row of empty wooden bench-like seats in the large, 
N|               dimly-lit, high-ceilinged room. Finally, in the first row, 
N|               REGGIE and SYLVIE are discovered. Besides GRANDPIERRE, they 
N|               are the only ones present. REGGIE turns around to look at 
N|               the empty room. They speak in whispers.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         It's not exactly what I'd call a 
U|                         large turn-out.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         Didn't Charles have any friends?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Don't ask me -- I'm only the widow.
P|                              (indicating GRANDPIERRE)
U|                         If Charles had died in bed we wouldn't 
U|                         even have him.
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         At least he knows how to behave at 
U|                         funerals.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- GRANDPIERRE
O|
N,G|               His eyes still lowered. CAMERA PANS DOWN to feature his hands -- 
N|               he is methodically trimming his nails with a small clipper.
O|
N,G|               TWO SHOT -- SYLVIE AND REGGIE
O|
C|                                     SYLVIE
U|                         Have you no idea who could have done 
U|                         it?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Until two days ago all I really knew 
U|                         about Charles was his name -- now it 
U|                         turns out I didn't even know that.
O|
N|               The front DOOR of the Chapel is heard opening and a shaft of 
N|               daylight streams in. The WOMEN turn.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- CHAPEL DOOR
O|
N|               The short, heavy-set figure of a MAN is outlined against the 
N|               bright outdoor light. He stands for a moment, then closes 
N|               the door after him. LEOPOLD GIDEON, short-sighted, bald, in 
N|               his middle forties, glances around nervously, like a barnyard 
N|               bird. Then he walks down one of the side aisles of the Chapel.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- GRANDPIERRE
O|
N|               As he watches GIDEON.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- REGGIE
O|
N|               As she watches him.
O|
