S|               INT. NIGHTCLUB -- NIGHT
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- EMCEE.
O|
N|               He stands on the dance floor in front of a five piece Latin 
N|               dance band, a spotlight on him, wearing his professional 
N|               smile as he speaks into a mike.
O|
C|                                     EMCEE
U|                         Bonsoir mesdames et messieurs, good 
U|                         evening ladies and gentlemen, guten 
U|                         Abend, meine Damen und Herren -- ce 
U|                         soir, comme tous les soirs, 
U|                         l'attraction ici, au Black Sheep 
U|                         Club, c'est vous! Venez, mesdames et 
U|                         messieurs, step right up, ladies and 
U|                         gentlemen, kommen Sie her, meine 
U|                         Damen en Herren, avanti, signore e 
U|                         signori -- avanti!
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- REGGIE AND PETER
O|
N|               At their table. REGGIE is dressed in a lovely Givenchy dress.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         What was all that?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Fun and games. Evidently we're the 
U|                         floorshow.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         You mean you and me?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         No, everyone. Come on -- avanti, 
U|                         avanti!
O|
N|               She rises and pulls him along.
O|
N,G|               WIDE ANGLE
O|
N,G|               Including the dance floor as most of the patrons go to it, 
N|               laughing self-consciously and looking around.
O|
C|                                     EMCEE
U|                         Écoutez bien -- les règles sont tres 
U|                         simples -- the rules are very easy -- 
U|                         deux équipes -- two teams -- each 
U|                         with one orange -- une orange -- 
U|                         eine apfelsine -- un' arrancia -- 
U|                         held under the chin, like so --
P|                              (does it)
U|                         comme ça -- and passed to the player 
U|                         behind you -- sans vous servir de 
U|                         vos mains -- using nothing but the 
U|                         chin -- no hands -- and keeping the 
U|                         orange at all costs from touching 
U|                         the floor. Commencez, Mesdames et 
U|                         Messieurs -- begin, ladies and 
U|                         gentlemen -- signore e signori, 
U|                         comminciate!
O|
N|               The EMCEE now circulates, forming teams, telling the patrons 
N|               to line up, making sure there is a woman next to every man. 
N|               REGGIE and PETER are the second couple in their line.
O|
N|               Then the EMCEE picks up a basket of oranges and places one 
N|               under the chin, held securely against the chest, of each man 
N|               at the head of the line. Blowing a whistle, a signal for the 
N|               game to begin and the band to play, the men turn to the women 
N|               behind them and attempt to transfer the oranges from under 
N|               their chins to under the chins of the women -- without using 
N|               their hands.
O|
N|               (This maneuver can only be accomplished by embracing one's 
N|               partner passionately and firmly pressing the orange against 
N|               the partner's throat until he or she can grip it tightly 
N|               enough with the chin to turn and offer it to the person next 
N|               in line, where the process begins anew. However, the slightest 
U|               miscalculation, which can be brought about by any number of 
N|               human frailties -- haste, modesty, inhibition or lack of co-
U|               ordination -- will surely result in losing control of the 
N|               orange so that it either falls to the floor [where it can 
N|               only be picked up by the chin] or it starts to roll and slide 
N|               from its proper place to some other, less proper, spot on 
U|               the human anatomy, forcing the man or the woman to retrieve 
U|               it -- again, with the chin only. This latter is an activity 
U|               which can prove extremely satisfying to old friends, or even 
U|               new friends who wish to become old friends, but can only be 
U|               a torment for total strangers and/or the English).
O|
N,G|               VARIOUS SHOTS -- ORANGE GAME
O|
N|               Some of the couples in various states of confusion, 
N|               entanglement and intimacy -- all of them, naturally, 
N|               hilarious.
O|
N,G|               TWO SHOT -- PETER AND GIRL
O|
N|               It is his turn to take the orange from a very short, but 
N|               quite shapely young girl in a strapless dress (held up by an 
N|               abundance of cantilever). PETER 'takes' when he sees the 
N|               twin obstacles which might -- and probably will -- encumber 
N|               the game but increase his worldly experience.
O|
N|               The contest begins: because of her stature he is forced to 
N|               move in low, making the ordinary embrace needed for success 
N|               difficult, if not impossible. Then, inexorably, the orange 
N|               starts to slip down the GIRL's front. Manfully he goes after 
N|               it.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- REGGIE
O|
N|               She is enjoying it thoroughly.
O|
N,G|               TWO SHOT -- PETER AND GIRL
O|
N|               Bending over backwards, in a sort of frontal half-nelson, 
N|               PETER makes a last valiant effort and voilà, grips the orange 
N|               under his chin -- amid much cheering and congratulations 
N|               from members of his TEAM.
O|
N|               Now he turns to REGGIE and they face one another for a moment.
O|
C|                                     PETER
U|                         En garde.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Lay on, MacDuff.
O|
N|               They go at it, working their bodies together to make it all 
N|               possible. Then, for a moment, the game and the onlookers 
N|               seem less important than their proximity. But, alas, they 
N|               are too good despite themselves and the transfer is 
N|               accomplished -- again with appreciative cheers from the TEAM.
O|
N|               REGGIE, with the orange now tucked firmly under her chin, 
N|               turns to the next team-member in line and is locked in an 
N|               embrace before she realizes her partner is LEOPOLD GIDEON, 
N|               the short, fat, balding man seen at the funeral and later in 
N|               BARTHOLOMEW's photo.
O|
N|               REGGIE starts to draw back but GIDEON holds her tightly.
O|
N|               Putting his chin around the orange he is able to speak quietly 
N|               in REGGIE's ear.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE TWO SHOT -- REGGIE AND GIDEON
O|
N|               Her eyes show her fright as he whispers:
O|
C|                                     GIDEON
U|                         Mrs. Lampert --
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         What do you want?
O|
C|                                     GIDEON
U|                         Didn't Charles tell you, Mrs. Lampert?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Tell me what?
O|
C|                                     GIDEON
U|                         It doesn't belong to you, Mrs. Lampert -- 
U|                         you do know that, don't you?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I don't know anything.
O|
C|                                     GIDEON
U|                         Mrs. Lampert, any morning now you 
U|                         could wake up dead.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Leave me alone -- !
O|
C|                                     GIDEON
U|                         Dead, Mrs. Lampert -- like last week's 
U|                         news -- like Charles, Mrs. Lampert --
O|
C|                                     REGGIE (SHOUTING)
U|                         Stop it!
O|
