U|                         what I called to tell you was there's 
U|                         someone else -- someone who wasn't 
U|                         in that photograph you showed me. He 
U|                         says his name is Peter Joshua -- but 
U|                         it isn't -- it's Dyle.
P|                              (a pause)
U|                         Mr. Bartholomew? -- are you still 
U|                         there?
O|
S|               INT. BARTHOLOMEW'S APARTMENT -- NIGHT
O|
N|               BARTHOLOMEW on the phone. He is silent for a moment, his 
N|               face troubled.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         I don't know who this Mr. Dyle is, 
U|                         but it's just possible we were wrong 
U|                         about who killed your husband.
O|
S|               INT. REGGIE'S ROOM -- NIGHT
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (on the phone)
U|                         You mean he might have -- Mr. 
U|                         Bartholomew, I'm catching the next 
U|                         plane out of here -- I'm not going 
U|                         to sit here and wait for someone to 
U|                         make chopped liver out of me!
O|
D|               DELETED
O|
S|               INT. BARTHOLOMEW'S APARTMENT -- NIGHT
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
P|                              (on the phone)
U|                         Where are you now -- can you meet 
U|                         me? Do you know Les Halles?
O|
S|               INT. REGGIE'S ROOM -- NIGHT
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (on the phone)
U|                         Yes, where?
P|                              (a pause)
U|                         -- in fifteen minutes. I'll be there.
O|
D|               DELETED
O|
S|               INT. REGGIE'S ROOM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE hangs up the phone, picks up her bag, checks her hair 
N|               in the mirror, then starts for the door. She stops as she 
N|               notices the connecting door leading to the room next door, 
N|               DYLE's room. She goes to it, silently slips out the key and 
N|               bends to peer through the keyhole.
O|
S|               INT. DYLE'S ROOM -- NIGHT (THROUGH KEYHOLE)
O|
N|               DYLE is removing his coat. Before he lays it over a chair, 
N|               he takes a gun from the inside pocket, checks it, and tucks 
N|               it into his belt.
O|
S|               INT. REGGIE'S ROOM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE reacts in surprise and fright, jumps quickly away 
N|               from the door. She hurries to the door leading to the hall 
N|               and reaches for the knob.
O|
S|               INT. DYLE'S ROOM -- NIGHT
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- ROOM KEY. The thread attached to it is pulled 
N|               (by the action of REGGIE's door opening) and the key falls 
N|               to the floor with a clatter.
O|
N,G|               WIDE ANGLE
O|
N,G|               Including DYLE as he reacts, his head wheeling to look at 
N|               the key. Snatching his coat, he runs for the door.
O|
S|               INT. HOTEL CORRIDOR -- THIRD LANDING -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE sneaks past DYLE's door. When she has passed, the 
N|               door opens and DYLE appears. REGGIE takes off on the run, 
N|               turning the corner and starting down the stairs.
O|
C|                                     DYLE
U|                         Reggie -- !
O|
N|               He starts after her.
O|
S|               INT. HOTEL LOBBY -- NIGHT
O|
N|               It is deserted, except for the sleeping NIGHT PORTER, as 
N|               REGGIE comes running down the stairs.
O|
C,E|                                     DYLE'S VOICE (O.S.)
U|                         Reggie... !
O|
N|               She turns, looking back towards the sound of his voice, but 
N|               does not slacken her speed. She runs out the front door.
O|
S|               EXT. HOTEL ENTRANCE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE runs out. She looks up the street, sees a TAXI and 
N|               hails it.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Taxi -- !
O|
N|               It pulls over to the curb. Looking once more over her shoulder 
N|               she takes a bill out of her pocket, opens the cab door, slams 
N|               it loudly without getting in and hands the bill to the driver.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         N'importe où -- vite! Allez-y!
O|
N|               She jumps back into the shadows of a nearby doorway as the 
N|               TAXI pulls away. At the same time DYLE runs out of the hotel.  
