U|                         along? Why didn't you tell me?
O|
N|               BARTHOLOMEW looks at her for a moment, then glances around; 
N|               his attention is drawn inside the doorway.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         It's enough to make you a vegetarian, 
U|                         isn't it?
O|
S|               INT. LES HALLES BUTCHERS' SHED -- NIGHT
O|
N|               Almost as far as the eye can see, row upon row of beef sides, 
N|               hung on hooks.
O|
N,G|               TWO SHOT -- REGGIE AND BARTHOLOMEW (TRAVELING)
O|
N|               As REGGIE looks at the hanging beef.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         It's just lucky that I'm not hanging 
U|                         next to one of those things right 
U|                         now.
O|
N|               She shudders, throws away her banana and turns back to 
N|               BARTHOLOMEW.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Mr. Bartholomew -- why didn't you 
U|                         tell me you knew about Dyle?
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         I didn't see any point. Dyle's dead.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Dead? Mr. Bartholomew -- maybe you'd 
U|                         better tell me what this thing's all 
U|                         about.
O|
D|               DELETED
O|
S|               INT. LES HALLES BISTRO -- NIGHT
O|
N|               Lined up at a zinc bar are several BUTCHERS, their white 
N|               smocks stained with blood. REGGIE and BARTHOLOMEW sit at the 
N|               table.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         I suppose you're old enough to have 
U|                         heard of World War Two?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Barely, yes.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         In 1944, five members of the O.S.S. -- 
U|                         the military espionage unit -- were 
U|                         ordered behind the German lines for 
U|                         the purpose of delivering $250,000 
U|                         in gold to the French Underground. 
U|                         The five men --
O|
N|               A WAITER arrives.
O|
C|                                     WAITER
U|                         Vous désirez?
O|
