N|               and lights it.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         Everything went smoothly enough until 
U|                         after the gold was buried -- then, 
U|                         before they could get out, they were 
U|                         ambushed by a German patrol. A machine 
U|                         gun separated Scobie from his right 
U|                         hand -- and caught Carson Dyle full 
U|                         in the stomach.
O|
N|               REGGIE takes another cigarette from his pack, rips off the 
N|               filter (he winces again) and puts it into her mouth.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         What's wrong with that one?
O|
N|               He points to the cigarette she just lit, still practically 
N|               brand-new in the ashtray.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Oh. Nothing, I guess. What happened 
U|                         then?
O|
N|               She hands over the newer one to BARTHOLOMEW, who sadly 
N|               examines its mutilated end while REGGIE returns to the first 
N|               cigarette.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         Have you any idea what these things 
U|                         cost over here?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Please go on, Mr. Bartholomew -- 
U|                         what happened then?
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         Scobie was able to travel, but Carson 
U|                         Dyle was clearly dying, so they --
O|
N|               The WAITER returns with the coffee and onion soup.
O|
C|                                     WAITER
U|                         La soupe, c'est pour qui?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Pour moi. Go on, Mr. Bartholomew.
O|
N|               The WAITER puts down the cup and bowl and leaves.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         Carson was dying so they were forced 
U|                         to leave him. They finally got back 
U|                         to the base, made their report, and 
U|                         waited for the war to end. Only 
U|                         Charles couldn't wait quite as long 
U|                         as the others. He beat them back to 
U|                         the gold, took everything for himself 
U|                         and disappeared. It's taken Gideon, 
U|                         Tex and Scobie all this time to catch 
U|                         up with him again.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         But if they stole all that money -- 
U|                         why can't you arrest them?
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         We know what happened from the bits 
U|                         and pieces we were able to paste 
U|                         together -- but we still have no 
U|                         proof.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         But what has all this got to do with 
U|                         the C.I.O.?
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         C.I.A., Mrs. Lampert. We're an 
U|                         extension of the wartime O.S.S. It 
U|                         was our money and we want it back.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I'm sorry, Mr. Bartholomew, but 
U|                         nothing you've told me has changed 
U|                         my mind. I still intend leaving Paris -- 
U|                         tonight.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         I wouldn't advise that, Mrs. Lampert.  
U|                         You'd better consider what happened 
U|                         to your husband when he tried to 
U|                         leave. Those men won't be very far 
U|                         away -- no matter where you go. In 
U|                         fact, I don't even see any point in 
U|                         your changing hotels. Please help 
U|                         us, Mrs. Lampert. Your government is 
U|                         counting on you.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Well, if I'm going to die, I might 
U|                         as well do it for my country.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         That's the spirit.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Oh, stop it. What do you want me to 
U|                         do?
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         We're anxious to know who this man 
U|                         is -- the one calling himself Dyle.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Maybe he really is Dyle. He could 
U|                         still be alive.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         No, Mrs. Lampert.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         But no one actually saw him die.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         No, Mrs. Lampert. His death is 
U|                         registered with the War Department 
U|                         in Washington.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Oh. Then who's this one?
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         I don't know -- but I think you'd 
U|                         better find out, don't you?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Me? Why me?
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         You're in an ideal position -- he 
U|                         trusts you.
