U|                         What about the blue one?
O|
C|                                     FÉLIX
U|                         It is called 'The Hawaiian Blue' and 
U|                         there are only seven left. In 1894 
U|                         the owner of one was murdered by a 
U|                         rival collector who was obsessed to 
U|                         own it.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         What's its value today?
O|
C|                                     FÉLIX
U|                         In human life? In greed? In suffering?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         In money.
O|
C|                                     FÉLIX
U|                         Forty-five thousand.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (to SYLVIE)
U|                         Do you have anything to eat?
P|                              (to FÉLIX)
U|                         And the orange one -- what about the 
U|                         orange one?
O|
C|                                     FÉLIX
U|                         A two-penny Mauritius -- issued in 
U|                         1856. Not so rare as the others -- 
U|                         $30,000 perhaps.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         And the last one?
O|
C|                                     FÉLIX
U|                         The best for the last -- le chef-
U|                         d'oeuvre de la collection. The 
U|                         masterpiece. It is the most valuable 
U|                         stamp in the world. It is called 
U|                         'The Gazette Guyanne.' It was printed 
U|                         by hand on colored paper in 1852 and 
U|                         marked with the initials of the 
U|                         printer.
P|                              (looking at it through 
P|                              the glass)
U|                         Today it has a value of $100,000.
P|                              (a pause)
U|                         Eh, bien -- I am not a thief. I knew 
U|                         there was some mistake. Take them.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (hesitating)
U|                         You gave the boy quite a lot of stamps 
U|                         in return, Monsieur Félix -- are 
U|                         they for sale now?
O|
C|                                     FÉLIX
P|                              (looking at the large 
P|                              bag)
U|                         Let me see. There are 350 European, 
U|                         200 Asian, 175 American, 100 African 
U|                         and twelve Princess Grace 
U|                         commemorative -- which comes to nine 
U|                         francs fifty.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (fishing money from 
P|                              her purse)
U|                         Here's ten.
O|
N|               FÉLIX goes to his wallet for the change.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Please keep it.
O|
C|                                     FÉLIX
U|                         I am a tradesman, Madame, not a 
U|                         doorman. And don't forget these.
O|
N|               He hands her the four stamps and her change.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I'm -- I'm sorry.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- FÉLIX
O|
C|                                     FÉLIX
U|                         No. For a few minutes they were mine -- 
U|                         that is enough.
O|
S|               INT. HOTEL CORRIDOR -- THIRD FLOOR LANDING -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE comes hurrying up the stairs. She goes first to 
N|               ADAM's room and knocks.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Adam? Adam? It's me, Reggie -- !
O|
N|               There is no answer. She goes to her own door and, to her 
N|               surprise, finds it an inch or two ajar.
O|
S|               INT. REGGIE'S ROOM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE enters. She freezes, having seen something on the 
N|               floor.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- TEX
O|
N|               His dead body lies on the floor, the wrists of his extended 
N|               arms tied to the leg of the bed, his ankles to the steam 
N|               radiator. And tied around his head is a plastic, transparent 
N|               bag, inside of which the suffocated man's face, the eyes 
N|               bulging against the plastic clinging tight to his features, 
N|               can be seen all too clearly. REGGIE enters the shot, bending 
N|               down to see if he's alive. Then she sees something beside 
N|               his hands near the leg of the bed.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- CARPET
O|
N|               With his dying effort, TEX has traced a name against the 
N|               grain of the maroon carpet -- 'DYLE.'
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- REGGIE
O|
N|               Astonished and horrified.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (gasping)
U|                         Dyle --
O|
N,G|               WIDER ANGLE
O|
N|               As she gets to her feet and hurries to the phone.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (on the phone)
U|                         Hello -- Balzac 30-04, s'il vous 
U|                         plait --
P|                              (waiting)
U|                         Mr. Bartholomew! Thank God you're 
U|                         there! Tex is dead, Mr. Bartholomew -- 
U|                         smothered -- and Adam did it -- he 
U|                         killed them all!
O|
S|               INT. BARTHOLOMEW'S APARTMENT -- NIGHT
O|
N|               BARTHOLOMEW, his face lathered for a shaving, is on the phone.
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
U|                         Just a minute, Mrs. Lampert -- you'd 
U|                         better give that to me slowly. Who's 
U|                         Adam?
O|
S|               INT. REGGIE'S ROOM -- NIGHT
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (on the phone)
U|                         The one who said he was Dyle's brother -- 
U|                         of course I'm sure -- Tex wrote the 
U|                         word 'Dyle' before he died. He's the 
U|                         murderer I tell you -- he's the only 
U|                         one left! You've got to do something!
O|
S|               INT. BARTHOLOMEW'S APARTMENT -- NIGHT
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
P|                              (on the phone)
U|                         Calm down, Mrs. Lampert -- please. 
U|                         Does he have the money?
O|
S|               INT. REGGIE'S ROOM -- NIGHT
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (on the phone)
U|                         No, I do -- it was the stamps on 
U|                         that letter Charles had with him on 
U|                         the train. They were in plain sight 
U|                         all the time, but no one ever bothered 
U|                         looking at the envelope.
O|
S|               INT. BARTHOLOMEW'S APARTMENT -- NIGHT
O|
C|                                     BARTHOLOMEW
P|                              (on the phone)
U|                         The envelope -- imagine that.  Mrs. 
U|                         Lampert, listen to me -- you're not 
U|                         safe as long as you've got these 
U|                         stamps. Go to the Embassy right away -- 
U|                         wait, I'd better meet you halfway -- 
U|                         it's quicker. Now, let's see -- do 
U|                         you know the center garden at the 
U|                         Palais Royal? -- yes, by the colonnade -- 
U|                         as soon as you can get there. Hurry, 
U|                         Mrs. Lampert.
O|
S|               INT. REGGIE'S ROOM -- NIGHT
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (on the phone)
U|                         Yes, I'm leaving now -- goodbye.
O|
N|               She hangs up, looks briefly at TEX's body, shudders, then 
N|               hurries to the door.
O|
S|               INT. HOTEL CORRIDOR -- THIRD LANDING -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE leaves her room and goes to the elevator. She 
N|               presses the button, then notices it is in use. She goes to 
N|               the stairs and starts down.
O|
S|               INT. HOTEL STAIRCASE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               Between the landings. The stairs curve around the open 
N|               elevator shaft. As REGGIE comes down the stairs, the cage 
N|               rises into view. Inside is ADAM. For a moment, she stops and 
N|               their eyes meet.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie -- the stamps -- what've you 
U|                         done with --?
O|
N|               REGGIE starts running downstairs.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Where are you going? Wait!
O|
N|               ADAM pushes the emergency stop button and then starts the 
N|               cage down.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie!
O|
S|               INT. HOTEL CORRIDOR -- SECOND LANDING -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE comes off the stairs, passes the elevator gate and 
N|               starts down toward the lobby, the cage a few feet behind 
N|               her.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie!
O|
S|               INT. HOTEL CORRIDOR -- FIRST LANDING -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE continues to run.
O|
S|               INT. HOTEL STAIRWAY -- NIGHT
O|
N|               Between the first landing and the lobby. REGGIE running, the 
N|               elevator following.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie -- stop!
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Why? So you can kill me too? Tex is 
U|                         dead, I've seen him! He said Dyle 
U|                         did it!
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         I'm not Dyle -- you know that!
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         But Tex didn't -- he still thought -- 
U|                         !
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Don't be an idiot!
O|
S|               INT. HOTEL LOBBY -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE reaches the lobby first and, without hesitation, races 
N|               toward the front door and out. The confused hotel MANAGER 
N|               behind the desk can only stare in surprise. The elevator, 
N|               ADAM inside, has not yet reached the bottom.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie -- ! I want those stamps!
O|
S|               EXT. HOTEL LOBBY -- NIGHT
O|
N|               A taxi stands by the curb. REGGIE leaves the hotel and runs 
N|               to it.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (indicating the 
P|                              direction)
U|                         Palais Royal -- vite!
O|
N|               Calmly, the DRIVER points to the little printed sign on his 
N|               windshield reading "ITALIE."
O|
C|                                     DRIVER
P|                              (pointing the other 
P|                              way)
U|                         Porte d'Italie, moi.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Mais c'est très vite! On veut me 
U|                         teur!
O|
C|                                     DRIVER
P|                              (shaking his head)
U|                         Italie.
O|
N|               She looks around and sees ADAM come out of the hotel and 
N|               straight toward her. She turns and runs off toward the Place 
N|               St. Michel.
O|
S|               EXT. PLACE ST. MICHEL -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE comes to the corner. She stops, sees the Métro 
N|               station ("St. Michel") and rushes to it, scampering down the 
N|               stairs. ADAM is behind her.
O|
S|               INT. ST. MICHEL MÉTRO STATION -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE comes flying down the stairs and runs past the ticket 
N|               booth, fishing in her bag for her carnet (booklet of tickets), 
N,G|               casting a quick look behind her. CAMERA PANS QUICKLY TO ADAM 
N|               just coming off the stairs, who runs after her.
O|
D|               DELETED
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO TICKET GATE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE gets to the gate ahead of ADAM and manages to crowd 
N|               in front of some OTHERS about to pass through. Barely 
N|               stopping, she holds out her ticket to the GUARD to be punched, 
N|               then heads down the platform, still running. ADAM gets to 
N|               the gate but the GUARD stops him as he tries to pass through.
O|
C|                                     GUARD
U|                         Billet, Monsieur.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
P|                              (breathless)
U|                         I don't want to go anywhere -- I'm 
U|                         only trying --
O|
C|                                     GUARD
P|                              (pointing off)
U|                         Billet, Monsieur.
O|
N|               ADAM tries to look past him, to see REGGIE, but gives it up 
N|               and goes back toward the ticket booth, on the run.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO PASSAGEWAY -- NIGHT
O|
N,G|               CAMERA LEADING REGGIE as she runs -- the passageway is nearly 
N|               empty. Her footsteps echo against the tile and concrete walls.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- PASSAGEWAY WALL (TRAVELING)
O|
N|               The jumble of advertising posters as it passes rapidly, 
N|               forming a moving band of letters, women, cartoons and colors.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO PASSAGEWAY -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE stops and pauses for a moment at a sign indicating 
N|               two different directions, an arrow for each.
O|
S|               "DIRECTION: Pte D'ORLÉANS Pte DE CLIGNANCOURT-------"
O|
N,G|               Choosing "Clignancourt," she runs off. CAMERA PANS SHARPLY, 
N,G|               180 degrees, to pick up ADAM rounding the corner in hot 
N|               pursuit.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO PLATFORM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE starts down the platform, looking behind her every 
N|               few steps.  Suddenly she looks up in surprise -- there, across 
N|               the tracks on the opposite platform is ADAM.  He has evidently 
N|               made the wrong turn back in the passageway.
O|
N|               They stare at each other for a moment.  Then the bell rings, 
N|               announcing the arrival of a train. ADAM turns, running back 
N|               through the exit behind him. Not knowing what to do, REGGIE 
N|               looks into the darkness of the tunnel. The approaching train 
N|               can be heard.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (to herself)
U|                         Come on -- please --
O|
N|               She turns to look at the gate -- slowly, the pneumatic door 
N|               starts to close. As it does, the train roars into the station.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO PASSAGEWAY -- NIGHT
O|
N|               The gate can be seen slowly closing. ADAM runs to it, tries 
N|               to force it back but cannot. Finally, he jumps up and, 
N|               commando style, vaults over it.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO PLATFORM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE is just entering the red center car (the two on either 
N|               side are dark green). ADAM runs for the red car and just 
N|               manages to make it as the doors shut in unison, the latches 
N|               falling with a concerted click and the little whistle blowing 
N|               to inform the motor-man to depart. The train starts to move.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO CAR -- NIGHT
O|
N|               The entire length of the car separates ADAM and REGGIE.
O|
N|               For a moment, their eyes meet, then ADAM starts to weave his 
N|               way past the other PASSENGERS, on his way to her.
O|
N|               Suddenly, he is stopped. ADAM turns to see a TRAIN GUARD.
O|
C|                                     TRAIN GUARD
U|                         Billet, Monsieur.
O|
N|               ADAM shows him his yellow ticket and starts past him, but 
N|               again the TRAIN GUARD stops him.
O|
C|                                     TRAIN GUARD
U|                         Vous êtes dans le premier classe, 
U|                         Monsieur.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         What?
O|
C|                                     TRAIN GUARD
P|                              (heavy accent)
U|                         This car is for first class only -- 
U|                         you have a second-class ticket.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         But that's what they gave me.
O|
N|               He tries to pull away from the TRAIN GUARD and finds himself 
N|               staring into the serious face of a GENDARME.
O|
C|                                     GENDARME
U|                         Monsieur -- ?
O|
N|               ADAM looks at the GENDARME, then at REGGIE.
O|
S|               INT. "PALAIS-ROYAL" MÉTRO PLATFORM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As the TRAIN pulls in and comes to a stop.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO CAR -- NIGHT
O|
N|               The GENDARME opens the door for ADAM and escorts him out.
O|
N|               ADAM turns once more to look at REGGIE as he goes. She remains 
N|               in the car.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO PLATFORM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               The GENDARME gestures for ADAM to enter the green, second- 
N|               class car behind the red, first-class one. Reluctantly, ADAM 
N|               does.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO CAR -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As ADAM enters and goes to the door through which he can see 
N|               REGGIE in the car ahead. She is gone. Moving quickly, he 
N|               returns to the exit door and looks at the platform.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO PLATFORM -- NIGHT
O|
N,G|               From ADAM'S P.O.V. She is hurrying toward an exit marked 
N|               "SORTIE."
O|
N,G|               ANOTHER ANGLE
O|
N|               Featuring ADAM as he hurries from the car. He finds his way 
N|               blocked by FIVE NUNS in large, white butterfly hats.
O|
N|               It takes him a few precious seconds to work his way around 
N|               them.
O|
D|               DELETED
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO SORTIE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE has entered an area leading to the exit. But as she 
N|               reaches the stairway leading up to the street level, she is 
N|               confronted with an iron grill barring her way. She tries to 
N|               open it, but it is firmly padlocked. A sign hung on it reads 
N|               "FERMÉ LES WEEKENDS." She turns, desperately looking for 
N|               some way out.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO PLATFORM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               ADAM is off the train. He stands on the platform as the train 
N|               doors slam shut, the latches click, the whistle blows and 
N|               the train pulls out. He looks around in all directions, 
N|               looking for some sign of REGGIE. He spots the exit marked 
N,M|               "SORTIE" (the same one used by REGGIE) and starts toward it.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO SORTIE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As ADAM enters the deserted area. There is, miraculously, no 
N|               sign of REGGIE. He goes to the locked grill and tries it, 
N,G,M|               testing the padlock. CAMERA PANS to a phone booth (solid 
N,M,G|               door with a window in the upper half) and we see REGGIE's 
N,G|               hand reaching up to dial a number.
O|
S|               INT. PHONE BOOTH -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE sits on the floor of the booth, dialing.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (to herself, as she 
P|                              dials)
U|                         Balzac 3 - 0 - 0 - 4.
O|
N|               She holds the receiver to her ear. The number can be heard 
N|               ringing but no one answers. She hangs up and reaches for the 
N|               phone book, leafing through its pages.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Embassies -- embassies --
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO SORTIE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               ADAM stands for a minute, looking around, not knowing what 
N|               to do.
O|
S|               INT. PHONE BOOTH -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE has finished dialing her number and now pushes the 
