O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie -- please believe me!
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         No!
O|
N|               As REGGIE wearily gains the top, ADAM lunges for her. He 
N|               manages to grab her foot as he falls forward, but all he 
N|               winds up with is a shoe which has come loose in his hand.
O|
N|               REGGIE shrieks, then regaining her balance, continues running, 
N|               limping in her one shoe. ADAM scrambles to his feet and starts 
N|               after her again.
O|
S|               INT. MÉTRO TICKET BOOTH AREA -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE, still hobbling, runs through and toward the stairs, 
N,G|               leading to the street. CAMERA PANS TO ADAM, as he, too, runs 
N|               through. He is again several yards behind her.
O|
S|               EXT. PLACE PALAIS ROYAL -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As REGGIE comes up the stairs from the Métro. She stops long 
N|               enough to kick off her other shoe, then runs across the 
N|               street, ignoring the traffic, toward the Rue de Valois (which 
N|               forms one side of the Palais Royal). ADAM is gaining on her 
N|               again.
O|
S|               EXT. PALAIS ROYAL COURTYARD -- COLONNADE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               The smaller court at the Comédie-Française end of the Palais 
N|               gardens, separated from the larger garden by a double 
N|               peristyle consisting of two twin rows (these separated from 
N|               each other by a small marble court) of twenty columns each -- 
N|               in all, eighty columns. The only person in sight is the man 
N|               we have known as BARTHOLOMEW, waiting at the far end of the 
N|               columns, looking at his watch impatiently.
O|
N|               Then, from the Rue de Valois side of the Palais, REGGIE runs 
N|               into the court. She spots "BARTHOLOMEW" and fishes in her 
N|               bag for the stamps as she runs, taking them out and waving 
N|               them.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Mr. Bartholomew -- he's chasing me!
O|
N|               ADAM has run into the court and now skids to a stop at the 
N|               near end of the colonnade as he spots "BARTHOLOMEW." REGGIE, 
N|               still running, is halfway between the two men. "BARTHOLOMEW" 
N|               draws his gun but can't get a shot at ADAM, who has ducked 
N|               in among the columns.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie -- stop! That's Carson Dyle!
O|
N|               This news hits REGGIE hard and she stops, in alarm.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (breathless)
U|                         Carson -- ?
O|
N|               She looks at "BARTHOLOMEW," then back at ADAM, who has drawn 
N|               his own gun.
O|
N|               (NOTE: Both "BARTHOLOMEW" and ADAM are in among the stone 
N|               columns at opposite ends of the colonnade, keeping out of 
N|               each other's sight. REGGIE stands out in the open, the stamps 
N|               in her hand, confused as to which man she should go to).
O|
C|                                     "BARTHOLOMEW"
P|                              (calmly)
U|                         We all know Carson Dyle is dead, 
U|                         Mrs. Lampert.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         It's Carson Dyle, I tell you!
O|
C|                                     "BARTHOLOMEW"
U|                         You're not going to believe him, 
U|                         Mrs. Lampert -- it's too fantastic. 
U|                         He's trying to trick you again.
O|
N|               REGGIE looks at one, then the other, not knowing what to do.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Tex recognized him -- that's why he 
U|                         said Dyle. If you give him those 
U|                         stamps, he'll kill you too!
O|
N|               REGGIE takes a step toward ADAM.
O|
C|                                     "BARTHOLOMEW"
U|                         Mrs. Lampert -- if I'm who he said, 
U|                         what's preventing me from killing 
U|                         you right now?
O|
N|               REGGIE stops, turns back to "BARTHOLOMEW."
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Because he'd have to come out to get 
U|                         the stamps -- he knows he'd never 
U|                         make it.
O|
C|                                     "BARTHOLOMEW"
U|                         What's the matter with you, Mrs. 
U|                         Lampert? Are you going to believe 
U|                         every lie he tells you? He wants the 
U|                         money for himself -- that's all he's 
U|                         ever wanted.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (to ADAM, explaining)
U|                         He's -- with the C.I.A. -- I've seen 
U|                         him at the Embassy.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Don't be a fool! He's Carson Dyle!
O|
C|                                     "BARTHOLOMEW"
U|                         That's right, Mrs. Lampert -- I'm a 
U|                         dead man -- look at me.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I don't know who anybody is any more!
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie -- listen to me!
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         You lied to me so many times --
O|
C|                                     ADAM
P|                              (gently)
U|                         Reggie -- trust me once more -- 
U|                         please.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Can I really believe you this time, 
U|                         Adam?
O|
C|                                     ADAM
P|                              (a pause)
U|                         There's not a reason on earth why 
U|                         you should.
O|
N|               She looks toward ADAM for a moment, then back to 
N|               "BARTHOLOMEW", then slowly starts toward ADAM.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         All right, Adam.
O|
C|                                     "BARTHOLOMEW"
U|                         Stop right now, Mrs. Lampert, or 
U|                         I'll kill you.
O|
N|               REGGIE stops in alarm.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         It won't get you the stamps, Dyle -- 
U|                         You'll have to come out to get them, 
U|                         and I'm not likely to miss at this 
U|                         range.
O|
C|                                     "BARTHOLOMEW"
P|                              (now called CARSON)
U|                         Maybe not -- but it takes a lot of 
U|                         bullets to kill me. They left me 
U|                         there with five of them in my legs 
U|                         and my stomach -- they knew I was 
U|                         still alive but they left me. I spent 
U|                         ten months in a German camp -- with 
U|                         nothing to stop the pain and no food -- 
U|                         they were willing to take all these 
U|                         chances for the money, but not for 
U|                         me. They deserved to die!
O|
M|               MED. SHOT -- ADAM
O|
N|               During the following, he looks around, looking for some way 
O|               out.
O|
C,E|                                     REGGIE'S VOICE (O.S.)
U|                         But I didn't have anything to do 
U|                         with --
O|
C,E|                                     CARSON'S VOICE (O.S.)
U|                         You've got the money. It belongs to 
U|                         me now! Please believe me, Mrs. 
U|                         Lampert -- I'll kill you -- a little 
U|                         more blood won't matter.
O|
N|               During this ADAM has moved out from behind the columns, 
N|               creeping cautiously across the open space between the two 
N|               colonnades and finally, behind the second.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- REGGIE AND CARSON
O|
C|                                     CARSON
U|                         I'll give you five to make up your 
U|                         mind, Mrs. Lampert.
O|
N|               She has seen ADAM's move from her angle, but doesn't know 
N|               quite what to do.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Wait, please! I need some time to 
U|                         think!
O|
C|                                     CARSON
U|                         One --
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- ADAM
O|
N|               As he slowly moves along behind the second colonnade, his 
N|               gun ready, trying to get an angle on CARSON.
O|
C,E|                                     CARSON'S VOICE (O.S.)
U|                         -- two --
O|
N|               Suddenly ADAM stops -- he has caught sight of CARSON through 
N|               the columns. But he will have a difficult shot.
O|
C,E|                                     CARSON'S VOICE (O.S.)
U|                         -- three --
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- CARSON
O|
C|                                     CARSON
U|                         -- four --
O|
N,G|               CAMERA PANS DOWN to his gun. As his finger tightens on the 
N|               trigger and the hammer moves slowly back.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- REGGIE
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Adam -- please!
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- ADAM
O|
N|               As he aims carefully and fires.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- COLUMN
O|
N|               As the bullet creases it.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- CARSON
O|
N|               As the deflected bullet rips the shoulder of his coat, leaving 
N|               him unharmed. He wheels.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- ADAM
O|
N|               With CARSON in the b.g., who fires at him. ADAM ducks behind 
N|               the column as the bullet hits it and screams off.
O|
N|               Quickly, he peers back out and throws another shot.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- REGGIE
O|
N|               Seeing CARSON otherwise occupied, she turns and runs toward 
N|               the open stage door of the Comédie Française behind her.
O|
N|               (Beside the door is a poster announcing the forthcoming 
N|               schedule of presentations.)
O|
N,G|               ANOTHER ANGLE
O|
N,G|               Including CARSON who, seeing REGGIE running to the door, 
N|               turns and fires at her. But he is too late -- she is safely 
N|               inside. CARSON looks quickly back toward ADAM, then takes 
N|               off after REGGIE.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- ADAM
O|
N,G|               Over his shoulder we see a broken picture of CARSON running 
N|               toward the theatre door, flashing by the near and far columns.  
N|               ADAM tries to get a shot at him, but can't.
O|
N|               Finally he runs after him.
O|
S|               INT. COMÉDIE FRANÇAISE -- BACKSTAGE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As CARSON enters and slams the door behind him, locking it.
O|
S|               INT. COMÉDIE FRANÇAISE -- STAGE DOOR -- NIGHT
O|
N|               ADAM arriving at the door, bangs on it, then looks around, 
N|               frustrated. Several yards away he sees a short stairway 
N|               leading down to a door below the street level. He runs to 
N|               it, tries the door and enters.
O|
S|               INT. COMÉDIE FRANÇAISE -- ORCHESTRA -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As CARSON enters the auditorium and looks around.
O|
N,G|               CARSON'S P.O.V.
O|
N,G|               As the CAMERA SWEEPS the magnificent old theatre -- boxes, 
N|               seats, stage, but there is no sign of REGGIE.
O|
N,G|               ANOTHER ANGLE
O|
N|               As CARSON walks up the aisle checking between the rows of 
N|               seats.
O|
S|               INT. COMÉDIE FRANÇAISE -- TRAPROOM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               A large room, lit by a single bare bulb, under the stage.
O|
N|               ADAM appears, moving cautiously, gun ready. He creeps along 
N|               next to the wall, looking around at all the various scenic 
N|               pieces which fill the room.
O|
S|               INT. COMÉDIE FRANÇAISE -- STAGE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As CARSON moves carefully across the darkened stage near the 
N,G|               footlights, looking for REGGIE. At mid-stage, CAMERA PANS 
N,G|               DOWN to his feet, only a few inches from the prompter's box.  
N|               Inside, huddling down, is a terrified REGGIE, holding her 
N|               breath as she watches him.
O|
N,G|               ANOTHER ANGLE
O|
N|               As CARSON moves into the opposite wings, sees the light board 
N|               and throws on all the switches. The stage is bathed in light.
O|
N|               He returns to the stage.
O|
S|               INT. TRAPROOM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               ADAM is looking up, having heard the footsteps on the stage 
N|               over his head -- and hearing them now. He looks around and 
N|               sees a narrow, curving staircase leading up. He goes to it, 
N|               and, starting up, finds a door. He tries the knob -- the 
N|               door is locked.
O|
S|               INT. PROMPTER'S BOX -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE, cringing back from the bright light, notices the 
N|               doorknob turning. It makes a slight clicking sound.
O|
S|               INT. COMÉDIE FRANÇAISE -- STAGE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               CARSON, upstage, looking behind a piece of classic scenery, 
N|               hears the doorknob and turns suddenly.
O|
N,G|               CARSON'S P.O.V.
O|
N|               We catch a quick glimpse of REGGIE as she ducks down out of 
N|               sight.  Too late.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- CARSON
O|
C|                                     CARSON
U|                         All right, Mrs. Lampert. The game's 
U|                         over. Come out of there.
O|
N,G|               WIDER ANGLE
O|
N|               REGGIE does not appear.
O|
C|                                     CARSON
U|                         I don't want to kill you, Mrs. Lampert --
U|                         but I will --
O|
S|               INT. TRAPROOM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               ADAM comes down the stairs from the prompter's box and looks 
N|               up at the ceiling.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- CEILING
O|
N|               It is divided into thirty-six square sections, each numbered 
N|               and lettered -- from 1A to 6F. They are trapdoors.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- ADAM
O|
N|               He looks from the ceiling to a row of levers on one wall.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- LEVERS
O|
N|               Thirty-six of them, numbered and lettered to correspond to 
N|               the traps.
O|
S|               INT. COMÉDIE FRANÇAISE -- STAGE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               As CARSON takes a few steps towards the prompter's box, his 
N|               gun ready.
O|
C|                                     CARSON
U|                         Did you hear me, Mrs. Lampert -- ?
O|
S|               INT. PROMPTER'S BOX -- NIGHT
O|
N|               REGGIE huddled inside.
O|
S|               INT. TRAPROOM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               ADAM is listening carefully, trying to figure out where CARSON 
N|               is standing, watching the ceiling.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- TRAP
O|
N|               It is marked C-4.
O|
C,E|                                     CARSON'S VOICE (O.S.)
U|                         I won't wait much longer, Mrs. Lampert
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- ADAM
O|
N|               As he turns to the levers and reaches for the one marked C-
N|               4. He is about to pull it.
O|
S|               INT. COMÉDIE FRANÇAISE -- STAGE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               CARSON takes a few more steps forward.
O|
S|               INT. TRAPROOM -- NIGHT
O|
N|               ADAM stops himself from pulling the lever just in time. He 
N|               lets his held breath escape. He looks back at the ceiling.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- TRAP
O|
N,G|               The one marked C-4. As CARSON's voice is heard, CAMERA MOVES 
N,G|               to the next trap, marked D-4.
O|
C,E|                                     CARSON'S VOICE (O.S.)
U|                         I know you're in there, Mrs. Lampert --
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- ADAM
O|
N|               He looks at the lever marked D-4. He is perspiring heavily.
O|
N|               Now he slowly reaches for the lever.
O|
S|               INT. COMÉDIE FRANÇAISE -- STAGE -- NIGHT
O|
N|               CARSON is about to move closer to the prompter's box when 
N|               suddenly the stage under him opens and he plummets through 
N|               out of sight. At the same time we hear a shot.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE SHOT -- PROMPTER'S BOX
O|
N|               As REGGIE slowly peers out.
O|
N,G|               REGGIE'S P.O.V.
O|
N|               The empty stage, without being able to see the open trap 
N|               from this low angle.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- REGGIE
O|
N|               As she climbs out of the booth and, seeing the open trap 
N|               now, runs to it, looking down through it.
O|
N,G|               MED. SHOT -- OPEN TRAP
O|
N|               FROM ABOVE, over REGGIE's head. She can see CARSON sprawled 
N|               on the floor below, face down and dead. ADAM stands beside 
N|               the body, looking up at REGGIE and smiling.
O|
N,G|               ANOTHER ANGLE
O|
N|               As GRANDPIERRE and his TWO ASSISTANTS, guns drawn, walk onto 
N|               the stage from the wings. They go to the open trap and look 
N|               down at ADAM.
O|
C|                                     GRANDPIERRE
U|                         Mr. Dyle -- you are under arrest for 
U|                         the murders of Charles Lampert, Herman 
U|                         Scobie, Joseph Penthollow, Leopold 
U|                         Gideon, and whoever that is down 
U|                         there.
O|
N|               ADAM is surprised, then shakes his head.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie -- you'd better tell him. He 
U|                         wouldn't dare hit a girl.
O|
S|               EXT. RUE DE RIVOLI -- NIGHT
O|
N,G|               As a TAXI rolls by the arcades, CAMERA PANNING with it.
O|
S|               INT. TAXI -- NIGHT (PROCESS)
O|
N|               REGGIE and ADAM in the rear of the cab. REGGIE has one of 
N|               her feet in her hand, shoe off, rubbing it.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         You didn't have to chase me so hard --
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Here, give it to me.
O|
N|               He starts to take the foot but she pulls it back and offers 
N|               him the other one.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         That one's done -- start on this 
U|                         one.
O|
N|               He takes the foot and begins rubbing it.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I'm sorry I thought you were the 
U|                         murderer, Adam -- how did I know 
U|                         that he was as big a liar as you 
U|                         are?
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         And that's all the gratitude I get 
U|                         for saving your hide.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         The truth, now -- was it my hide -- 
U|                         or the stamps?
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         What a terrible thing to say. How 
U|                         could you even think that?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         All right, prove it to me -- tell me 
U|                         to go to the Embassy first thing in 
U|                         the morning and turn in those stamps.
O|
N|               ADAM says nothing.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         I said, tell me to go to the --
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         I heard you, I heard you.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Then say it.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Reggie -- listen to me --
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Never mind -- I'll go by myself.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         What makes you think they're even 
U|                         interested? It's only a quarter of a 
U|                         million -- it'll cost more than that 
U|                         to fix up their bookkeeping. As a 
U|                         taxpayer --
O|
S|               EXT. AMERICAN EMBASSY -- MAIN ENTRANCE -- DAY
O|
N|               As REGGIE and ADAM approach the MARINE in full-dress uniform 
N|               always on guard at the Embassy.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (to ADAM)
U|                         Who's a taxpayer? Crooks don't pay
U|                         taxes.  Excuse me, soldier --
O|
C|                                     MARINE
U|                         Marine, ma'am.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Forgive me. Whom would I see regarding 
U|                         the return of stolen Government money?
O|
C|                                     MARINE
U|                         You might try the Treasury Department, 
U|                         ma'am -- Room 216, second floor, Mr. 
U|                         Cruikshank.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Cruikshank, 216. Thank you, Marine.
O|
S|               INT. EMBASSY CORRIDOR -- DAY
O|
N|               Featuring a door marked "216." REGGIE and ADAM appear.
O|
C|                                     ADAM
U|                         Do you mind if I wait out here? The 
U|                         sight of all that money being given 
U|                         away might make me break out.
O|
S|               INT. EMBASSY TREASURY OFFICE -- DAY
O|
N|               A SECRETARY sits behind a desk. She looks up as REGGIE enters.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Mr. Cruikshank, please -- my name is 
U|                         Lampert.
O|
N|               The SECRETARY picks up her phone and presses a button.
O|
C|                                     SECRETARY
U|                         Mr. Cruikshank, a Miss --
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Mrs.
O|
C|                                     SECRETARY
U|                         -- a Mrs. Lampert to see you -- yes 
U|                         sir.
P|                              (to REGGIE)
U|                         Go right in.
O|
N|               REGGIE goes to the door leading to the private office.
O|
S|               INT. CRUIKSHANK'S OFFICE -- DAY
O|
N|               Featuring the door as REGGIE enters. She stops suddenly.
O|
N,G|               ANOTHER ANGLE
O|
N|               Featuring the desk. Behind it sits ADAM (now CRUIKSHANK).
O|
N|               REGGIE stares at him, unbelievingly, then looks around, 
N|               confused. By way of explanation he indicates the door to the 
N|               hall.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (blowing up)
U|                         Well, of all the mean, rotten, 
U|                         contemptible, crooked --
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         Crooked? I should think you'd be 
U|                         glad to find out I wasn't crooked.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         You couldn't even be honest about 
U|                         being dishonest. Why didn't you say 
U|                         something?
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         We're not allowed to tell. May I 
U|                         have the stamps, please?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (reaching into her 
P|                              bag)
U|                         Here --
P|                              (hesitating)
U|                         Wait a minute -- how did Carson Dyle 
U|                         get an office in here, anyway?
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         When did you see him -- what time, I 
U|                         mean?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Around one.
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         The lunch hour. He probably worked 
U|                         it out in advance. He found an office 
U|                         that was usually left open and just 
U|                         moved in for the time you were here.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Then how do I know this is your 
U|                         office?
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
P|                              (picking up the phone)
U|                         Mrs. Foster -- send a memo to 
U|                         Bartholomew at Security recommending 
U|                         that --
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Bartholomew?
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         -- recommending that all Embassy 
U|                         offices be locked during the lunch 
U|                         hour.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Starting with his own.
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
P|                              (hanging up)
U|                         Okay, now -- hand over those stamps.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         What's your first name today?
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         Brian.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Brian Cruikshank -- it would serve 
U|                         me right if I got stuck with that 
U|                         one.
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         Who asked you to get stuck with any 
U|                         of them?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Is there a Mrs. Cruikshank?
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         Yes.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         But you're -- divorced?
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         No.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
P|                              (crestfallen)
U|                         Oh.
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         My mother -- she lives in Detroit. 
U|                         Come on now -- give me those stamps.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Only if you can prove to me that 
U|                         you're really Brian Cruikshank.
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         How about if next week some time I 
U|                         put it on a marriage license -- that 
U|                         ought to --
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         Quit stalling -- I want to see some 
U|                         identification -- now!
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         I wouldn't lie on a thing like that -- 
U|                         I could go to jail.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         You'd lie about anything.
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         Well, maybe we'd better forget about 
U|                         it, then.
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         You can't prove it, can you? You're 
U|                         still trying to --
P|                              (the coin drops into 
P|                              the slot)
U|                         marriage license! Did you say -- ?
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         I didn't say anything. Will you give 
U|                         me those stamps?
O|
C|                                     REGGIE
U|                         You did too say it -- I heard you.  
U|                         Oh, I love you Adam -- I mean Alex -- 
U|                         er, Peter -- Brian. I hope we have 
U|                         lots of boys -- we can name them all 
U|                         after you.
O|
C|                                     CRUIKSHANK
U|                         Before we start on that, do you mind 
U|                         handing over the stamps?
O|
